From: GREG NEWTON <gnewton@ozemail.com.au>
Date: Sun, 2 Oct 94 20:39:55 +1000
Subject: [#94-10-13] Functional motivation for evolution?

Reply to message text:

  From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)

PC>The idea of consciousness, as something qualitatively separate from the
PC>behaviors we observe in animals, is very old.  It comes from ancient
PC>Greeks, from religious dogma, from Descartes, from our personal wishful
PC>thinking that something makes us special.  It comes from introspection
PC>- quite a deceptive source. 

PC>You dismiss the notion of a continuum of behavioral complexity while
PC>insisting that you understand what evolution is about.  How then, did this
PCmystical special property arise?  What governs the four humours?  Where is the
PC>aether?  (To attempt to avoid further misunderstanding, I'll make my sarcasm
PC>clearer.  I am likening your assertions of the special nature of 
consciousness
PC>in humans to the Greek's assertions about the four humours that govern the
PC>body's function, and to the 19th century physicist's search for the aether 
that
PC>permeates all space.  These faulty concepts were eventually discarded as the
PC>scientific process called into question the assumptions upon which they were
PC>based.  These difficulty was in finding these assumptions; they were so deep
PC>that nobody considered questioning them.  The same may be the case for
PC>consciousness.  I am insisting that we re-examine our assumptions; you insist
PC>that we accept them as incontrivertible.  Thus, metaphorically speaking, you
PC>insist on searching for the aether.)

If you haven't already read it I would recommend reading the thesis by Julian 
Jaynes of Princeton Uni titled "The Origin Of Consciousness in the Breakdown of 
the Bicameral Mind" - Published by Houghton Mifflin ISBN 0-395-32440-8 
(Paperback version).  It provides some very interesting insights into the whole 
area of human consciousness.

Regards, Greg.











